=0ASupplier Commit Dates Need a Confidence Score.=0A=0AEvery promised date =
carries different reliability. Most planning systems treat=0Athem as equall=
y solid.=0A=0ASupplier delivery performance is not improving. The ISM Manuf=
acturing Supplier=0ADeliveries Index registered 58.9 in July 2026, up from =
57.4 the prior month.=0AReadings above 50 indicate slower supplier deliveri=
es, and the index has stayed=0Aelevated through the summer. At the same tim=
e, 70% of organizations have had=0Abetween one and five critical suppliers =
fail in the past 12 months, and 41% say=0Athey are one supplier failure awa=
y from a supply chain crisis.=0A=0AThe problem is not that suppliers are be=
coming less reliable. The problem is=0Athat most operators discover supplie=
r failure only after it happens. 57% admit=0Athey are reacting to supplier =
failure rather than anticipating it, even though=0A73% say they would like =
to spot supplier distress earlier. The evidence to see=0Ait coming already =
exists. It just lives in scattered form: acknowledgment=0Atimestamps, PO ch=
ange logs, partial-shipment records, and informal follow-up=0Anotes that no=
body aggregates into one signal.=0A=0AEvidence already in your system=0A=0A=
=C2=B7 Organizations take 25 days on average to identify and onboard a repl=
acement=0Asupplier, and 74% are exposed to shortages or operational disrupt=
ion during that=0Awindow. The cost is not just the disruption. It is all th=
e customer promises=0Athat were made on top of dates that should not have b=
een trusted.=0A=0A=C2=B7 76% of organizations still rely on manual processe=
s for supplier due diligence=0Aand risk checks, and 58% say their reliance =
on spreadsheets exposes them to=0Ahuman error. Most never extract the suppl=
ier-reliability signals that are=0Aalready sitting in acknowledgment logs a=
nd PO histories.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Reliability now outweighs speed in keeping cus=
tomers. Retail delivery leaders=0Asay: "It's when you're not reliable is wh=
en we're going to lose our customers."=0AA 2024 McKinsey consumer survey fo=
und shoppers rank arriving within the promised=0Awindow above speed, with s=
peed falling from the top delivery priority in 2022 to=0Afifth in 2024. Ind=
ustrial buyers expect the same discipline from their own=0Asuppliers.=0A=0A=
The operating environment is getting less predictable. The tools to see it=
=0Acoming already exist in scattered form. The operators who assemble that =
evidence=0Afirst get to act before a date slips instead of scrambling after=
 it breaks.=0A=0AThe promised date is not a fact. It is the output of a pro=
cess that has its own=0Atrack record, and that track record is knowable bef=
ore the date is missed.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AEvery order=
 is now a rush order.=0A=0AAaron Bradley, VP of Sales & Compliance, Terry T=
own, on the cost of broken=0Adelivery trust=0A=0A"The way this industry mov=
es now, every order is a rush order. Once someone=0Aloses trust in that com=
munication or delivery time, the customer won't come=0Aback."=0A=0AAaron Br=
