selor, August 3, 2026=0A[https://members.asicentral.com/news/strategy/july-=
2026/counselor-state-of-the-industry-2026-becoming-indispensable-to-distrib=
utors/]=0A=0AThat is exactly the blind spot the confidence score below is b=
uilt to close. The=0Amoment a customer loses trust in a delivery promise is=
 the moment they start=0Alooking at a competitor. Seeing which promises are=
 already at risk before the=0Atrust is spent is the difference between acti=
ng as a supplier and reacting as a=0Avendor.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AThe ope=
rators winning on customer retention are not the ones with the best=0Aforec=
asts. They are the ones who spot which supplier commitments are already at=
=0Arisk before the customer discovers it through a missed shipment.=0A=0A=
=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Supplier Commit-Confidence Board.=0A=0AOn=
e score per open commitment. A weekly view of which dates deserve trust.=0A=
=0AA ranked list that tells the planner which dates to buffer, which to mon=
itor,=0Aand which to escalate now instead of after the date slips.=0A=0AEve=
ry open purchase order carries a promised date, and every promised date get=
s=0Aplanned against as if it were equally solid. It never is. Some supplier=
s move=0Adates the moment a PO is placed. Others acknowledge late, ship par=
tial, or have=0Aa track record that quietly degrades on exactly the part fa=
milies that matter=0Amost.=0A=0AThe Supplier Commit-Confidence Board turns =
that scattered evidence into one=0Ascore per open commitment. It does not r=
eplace the ERP or the supplier portal.=0AIt reads from them and produces a =
weekly view that tells the planner which dates=0Ato trust, which to buffer,=
 and which to escalate now instead of after the date=0Aslips.=0A=0AWhat goe=
s into it=0A=0ACreate one row for every open PO line with a promised delive=
ry date. Each row=0Acaptures:=0A=0A=C2=B7 Supplier and part. Supplier name,=
 part number or part family, and criticality=0Atier (single-source, long le=
ad-time, safety-related, or readily available with=0Abackup).=0A=0A=C2=B7 P=
romised date. The current committed date as it stands today, and the origin=
al=0Adate first committed at PO placement.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Acknowledgment lag. =
Days between PO issuance and supplier acknowledgment. Late=0Aor missing ack=
nowledgment is an early confidence signal, before any date has=0Amoved.=0A=
=0A=C2=B7 Date-change count. How many times this line's promised date has m=
oved since=0Athe PO was placed, and the direction and size of each change.=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Partial-shipment history. Whether this supplier has shipped pa=
rtial against=0Acomparable orders in the last 6-12 months, and how often a =
partial shipment=0Apreceded a final delay.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Recent on-time rate.=
 This supplier's on-time percentage over the trailing 90=0Aand 365 days, we=
ighted toward the same part family when volume allows.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Order co=
ndition flags. Rush order, first order with this supplier, order=0Aplaced d=
uring a known constraint, or unusually large quantity relative to=0Asupplie=
r's typical run size.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Confidence score. A single 1-100 score bu=
ilt from the fields above, weighted=0Aby part criticality so a low score on=
 a single-source critical part surfaces=0Aabove a low score on a readily av=
ailable commodity part.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Recommended action. Hold, monitor, cont=
act supplier, expedite alternate=0Asourcing, or escalate to customer commun=
ication.=0A=0AThe scoring logic=0A=0AKeep the scoring transparent enough th=
at a planner can explain any score in one=0Asentence. Start every commitmen=
t at a baseline score tied to that supplier's=0Atrailing on-time rate. Subt=
ract points for acknowledgment lag beyond the=0Asupplier's typical response=
 time. Subtract points for each date change, with=0Alater changes (closer t=
o the promised date) weighted more heavily than early=0Achanges. Subtract p=
oints if a partial shipment has already occurred on this line=0Aor is a doc=
umented pattern for this supplier. Multiply the resulting score by a=0Acrit=
icality weight so single-source and safety-related parts surface first=0Are=
gardless of raw score. Do not let the score replace judgment. It ranks=0Aat=
tention, it does not auto-generate customer promises.=0A=0ASupplier / Part=
