68=0A=0AMonitor=0A=0AWhat the weekly output looks like=0A=0AEvery Monday, t=
he planner receives one view with four blocks: a confidence heat=0Amap with=
 every open commitment sorted by score (lowest confidence and highest=0Acri=
ticality at the top); a movers list showing any commitment whose score=0Adr=
opped since last week and the specific field that changed; customer exposur=
e=0Aflagging which low-confidence commitments feed a specific customer prom=
ise; and=0Arecommended actions listing the three to five commitments that n=
eed a supplier=0Acall, an alternate-sourcing check, or a proactive customer=
 conversation this=0Aweek.=0A=0AOperating rhythm=0A=0ADaily: Automated fiel=
ds (acknowledgment status, date changes, partial-shipment=0Aflags) update f=
rom ERP and supplier-portal data without manual entry.=0A=0AMonday: Planner=
 reviews the confidence heat map and movers list in a focused=0A15-20 minut=
e session, not a full PO-by-PO review.=0A=0AMidweek: Any commitment that dr=
ops below a defined threshold triggers an=0Aimmediate supplier contact or c=
ustomer-facing heads-up, not a wait for Friday.=0A=0AMonthly: Compare predi=
cted low-confidence commitments against what actually=0Aslipped. Use the mi=
ss rate to retune the scoring weights.=0A=0AQuarterly: Review supplier-leve=
l trends. A supplier whose average confidence=0Ascore is declining across m=
ultiple parts is a sourcing conversation, not just a=0Aplanning adjustment.=
=0A=0ABuild guardrails=0A=0A=C2=B7 Score the commitment, not the supplier r=
elationship. A good supplier can still=0Ahave a low-confidence line on a sp=
ecific constrained part.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Keep the scoring logic visible to the =
planner. A black-box score that can't be=0Aexplained in one sentence will g=
et ignored the first time it's wrong.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Weight by criticality fir=
st. A perfect score on a commodity part matters less=0Athan a mediocre scor=
e on a single-source part.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Do not auto-communicate to customers=
. The score informs a human decision about=0Awhen and how to raise exposur=
e, it does not trigger the message itself.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Feed it from data yo=
u already have. Acknowledgment timestamps, PO change logs,=0Aand shipment r=
ecords already exist in most ERPs. This is an aggregation and=0Aweighting l=
ayer, not a new data-capture burden.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Retune quarterly. Supplier=
 behavior changes. A scoring model that never gets=0Achecked against actual=
 outcomes becomes another number nobody trusts.=0A=0AWhat the operator gets=
 every week=0A=0A=C2=B7 A ranked view of which promised dates deserve trust=
 and which deserve a=0Abuffer.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Early visibility into suppliers =
whose reliability is quietly declining, before=0Aa single missed date makes=
 it obvious.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A clear reason to start an alternate-sourcing or c=
ustomer conversation before=0Athe date slips, not after.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Less t=
ime spent manually re-checking every open PO and more time spent on the=0Ah=
andful that actually matter.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A record of which planning assumpt=
ions were wrong, so the model gets sharper=0Aevery cycle.=0A=0ABuild paths=
=0A=0APath 1. Internal build=0A=0AStart with a spreadsheet or Airtable base=
 pulling the fields above from your ERP=0Aexport: promised date, original d=
ate, acknowledgment timestamp, date-change=0Ahistory, and partial-shipment =
flags. Assign a criticality tier to your top=0A50-100 parts by hand. Build =
a simple weighted score, even a basic=0Apoint-deduction model, and sort by =
score every Monday. Track your miss rate for=0Aone quarter before trusting =
the score to drive customer conversations.=0A=0APath 2. ERP or partner path=
=0A=0AAsk your ERP provider or systems integrator whether commit-date histo=
ry,=0Aacknowledgment timestamps, and partial-shipment data are already capt=
ured and=0Aexportable. Most are, they're just never aggregated into a singl=
e view. The=0Adeliverable should include a per-line confidence score, a cri=
ticality weighting=0Ascheme, a weekly ranked view, and change-tracking so a=
